/// from: `android.bluetooth.BluetoothSocket`
///
/// A connected or connecting Bluetooth socket.
///
///The interface for Bluetooth Sockets is similar to that of TCP sockets: java.net.Socket
///and java.net.ServerSocket. On the server side, use a android.bluetooth.BluetoothServerSocket BluetoothServerSocket to
///create a listening server socket. When a connection is accepted by the android.bluetooth.BluetoothServerSocket BluetoothServerSocket, it will return a new android.bluetooth.BluetoothSocket BluetoothSocket to manage the connection. On
///the client side, use a single android.bluetooth.BluetoothSocket BluetoothSocket to both initiate an outgoing connection and
///to manage the connection.
///
///The most common type of Bluetooth socket is RFCOMM, which is the type supported by the Android
///APIs. RFCOMM is a connection-oriented, streaming transport over Bluetooth. It is also known as
///the Serial Port Profile (SPP).
///
///To create a android.bluetooth.BluetoothSocket BluetoothSocket for connecting to a known device, use android.bluetooth.BluetoothDevice\#createRfcommSocketToServiceRecord BluetoothDevice.createRfcommSocketToServiceRecord(). Then call \#connect() to attempt a
///connection to the remote device. This call will block until a connection is established or the
///connection fails.
///
///To create a android.bluetooth.BluetoothSocket BluetoothSocket as a server (or "host"), see the android.bluetooth.BluetoothServerSocket BluetoothServerSocket documentation.
///
///Once the socket is connected, whether initiated as a client or accepted as a server, open the
